  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Sat Oct 17 2026\n\nGood agreement between the various model solutions and ensembles\nthrough the extended period. The main concern through the\nextended period is a storm system slated for mid to late week\nimpacting mountain areas.\n\nSunday through Tuesday...persistent southwest flow will prevail\nacross southern Colorado into early next week. Two upper waves\nembedded in the flow will lead to isolated to scattered\nmountains showers and thunderstorms. Dry conditions will prevail\non Sunday as upper energy begins to lift out of Arizona. Sunday\ntemperatures will be warm, with mostly lower to mid 70s across\nthe Plains. An isolated shower may be possible across the San\nJuan Range by late Sunday afternoon as energy approaches.\n\nThe upper energy over Arizona will track northeast across\nColorado on Monday. Expected shower coverage to increase across\nthe Continental Divide Sunday night and continue through Monday\nafternoon. Not much cold air with this energy, and snow levels\nwill be high, above 12-13 kft. A quick inch or two may be\npossible above these elevations across the San Juans. As the\nupper wave tracks northeast, a few showers may spread northeast\ninto Teller County and the Palmer Divide Monday evening. Not\nmuch moisture to work with unfavorable southwest flow aloft will\nlikely limit precipitation to just a few light showers if\nanything at all. The Plains look to remain dry as this energy\nmoves northeast.  \n\nBy Tuesday, an upper low moves into the West Coast with moisture\nmoving northward out of the tropics. This will help prime the\narea for the upper storm system for mid to late week. A few\nlight showers will remain possible along the Continental Divide\nTuesday due to persistent southwest flow and embedded energy.\n\nA weak boundary will arrive on Monday, knocking 3 to 5 degrees \noff highs Monday afternoon. Temperatures will rebound on Tuesday\nback into the lower to mid 70s across the Plains.\n\nWednesday through Saturday...a strong upper level low is\nforecast to track slowly east from southern California,\nnortheast across Colorado for mid to late week. Plenty of deep\ntropical moisture with PWAT values of 0.8-1.1 inches will spread\nnorthward on Wednesday and prevail across Colorado Wednesday\ninto Thursday. Strong orographic moisture flow will lead to\nheavy precipitation along the Continental Divide, favoring the\nSan Juan Range and La Garita Mountains. Model guidance and\nensembles support heavy rainfall, with Wolf Creek Pass QPF \nprogs of 1-3 inches (the Canadian has higher amounts) during \nthis period. Again, not much cold air with this system, with \nsnow levels likely above 12-13 kft. There could be significant \nsnow accumulations for the high peaks of the San Juan, La Garita\nand southern Sangre de Cristo ranges. At this time, the \nsouthwest flow will likely keep showers west of I-25 during this\nperiod, with a majority of the Plains remaining dry. \nTemperatures on the Plains will remain mild with 70s to near 80 \nfor highs.\n\nFor the later part of the extended period, much will depend on\nhow this upper system ejects across the state. The ECMWF and\nCanadian have remain consistent, lifting the upper low away from\nColorado Friday into Saturday with a gradual drying trend. This\nwas similar to the 18z GFS. The 00z GFS reloads the upper low\nand sends a strong cold front south across the Plains on\nSaturday, with heavy precipitation with the cold front. The NBM\njust has widespread low POPS across the Plains, and did not\nstray from this given the uncertainty for Friday and Saturday.\n\nIf you are traveling across the Mountains Wednesday into\nThursday, be prepared for heavy precipitation, and very high\nelevation snowfall. This area has seen heavy rainfall over the\npast month, and could be prone to flash flooding and slides.",
